Subject: vhost_user: a request the backend handler refuses must not kill the device

The vhost-user epoll worker treats every error from `handle_request()` as
fatal: it stores `disconnected` and returns `EpollHelperError::HandleEvent`,
which terminates the worker thread. From that point the device is deaf. A
backend that is alive and well gets no answer to any later request, and its
guest hangs on the next one.

That is right for a broken socket and wrong for a refused request, and the
two are already distinguishable.

`FrontendReqHandler::handle_request()` (vhost 0.16) dispatches to the
handler, maps a handler failure to `Error::ReqHandlerError`, and then --
before returning it -- calls

    self.send_ack_message(&hdr, &res)?;

so the backend has ALREADY been told that its request failed, over the
protocol, and the two ends are still in step. Nothing about the channel is
broken. The frontend then throws the device away anyway.

`ReqHandlerError` is produced only by those `map_err` calls, i.e. only when
a handler said no. Every other variant means the socket is broken or the
stream is out of sync, and those stay fatal here.

The case that motivates it is SHMEM_MAP, and it is one this series created:
0001 bounds-checks `shmem_map` and `shmem_unmap` against the window,
"because a backend is not trusted to stay inside it". That check is correct
and it is reachable -- and before this patch, the first time it fires it
costs the whole device rather than the one mapping. A backend cannot recover
from it however carefully it handles the refusal, because there is no longer
anybody listening.

No behaviour changes for a backend that never has a request refused: the
`Ok` arm and every non-`ReqHandlerError` arm are what they were.

--- a/virtio-devices/src/vhost_user/mod.rs
+++ b/virtio-devices/src/vhost_user/mod.rs
@@ -405,14 +405,43 @@
             }
             BACKEND_REQ_EVENT => {
                 if let Some(backend_req_handler) = self.backend_req_handler.as_mut() {
-                    backend_req_handler
-                        .handle_request()
-                        .map(|_| ())
-                        .map_err(|e| {
-                            EpollHelperError::HandleEvent(anyhow!(
-                                "Failed to handle request from vhost-user backend: {e:?}"
-                            ))
-                        })
+                    match backend_req_handler.handle_request() {
+                        Ok(_) => Ok(()),
+                        // A request the HANDLER refused is not a broken
+                        // channel. `handle_request` has already answered the
+                        // backend for us -- it calls `send_ack_message` with
+                        // this very result before returning it -- so the
+                        // backend has been told its request failed and the
+                        // two ends are still in step.
+                        //
+                        // Treating it as fatal costs the whole device: the
+                        // error marks it disconnected and terminates the
+                        // epoll worker, so every later request from a backend
+                        // that is alive and well goes unanswered and its
+                        // guest hangs on the next one. A backend that asks
+                        // for something the VMM cannot do -- a SHMEM_MAP that
+                        // does not fit the window is the obvious one -- can
+                        // then never recover, however correct its own
+                        // handling of the refusal is.
+                        //
+                        // ReqHandlerError is the variant `handle_request`
+                        // produces for exactly this case, by `map_err` on
+                        // each handler call; every other variant means the
+                        // socket is broken or the stream is out of sync, and
+                        // those stay fatal.
+                        Err(VhostUserError::ReqHandlerError(e)) => {
+                            warn!(
+                                "vhost-user backend request refused for socket {}: {e:?} \
+                                 -- the backend has been sent the failure and the device \
+                                 stays up",
+                                self.socket_path
+                            );
+                            Ok(())
+                        }
+                        Err(e) => Err(EpollHelperError::HandleEvent(anyhow!(
+                            "Failed to handle request from vhost-user backend: {e:?}"
+                        ))),
+                    }
                 } else {
                     Ok(())
                 }
